      <section>
        <h2><span class="pin-name">04</span> Forgiving scoring</h2>
        <p>
          When you type your answers, a typo should not cost you a point, but anything should not pass
          either. Each answer is compared in four steps:
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>Both texts are simplified: lower case, no accents or punctuation, "&amp;" becomes "and".</li>
          <li>What varies between versions is removed: "(feat. X)", "- Remastered 2011".</li>
          <li>
            Their similarity is measured (Levenshtein distance): at 80% or more, the answer is worth
            10 points; at 55% or more, 5 points.
          </li>
          <li>
            For a song with several artists, each one is worth an equal share of the 10 points. For a year,
            the exact year is worth 10 points, one year off 6, three years off 3.
          </li>
        </ol>
        <p>The result is shared as squares, Wordle-style: 🟧 all right, 🟫 partly, ⬛ missed.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2><span class="pin-name">05</span> "Who listens to this?": a server that referees</h2>
        <p>
          With several players, no phone can be trusted on its own: the server draws the rounds, keeps the
          answers secret until the reveal, keeps time and scores. Each room is a Cloudflare Durable Object,
          which holds the game state and talks to every phone over WebSocket.
        </p>
        <p>A game, from start to finish:</p>
        <ol>
          <li>The host creates a room and gets a 4-letter code, with no I or O so it reads well aloud.</li>
          <li>Up to 8 players join; two identical nicknames become "Léa" and "Léa 2".</li>
          <li>Each player brings 15 of their songs, from any of the three sources.</li>
          <li>
            The server draws 10 to 15 rounds, the same number for each player, never more than three in a
            row with the same player's songs, spreading the artists.
          </li>
          <li>
            Each round, the snippet plays on every phone, or only on the host's when everyone is in the same
            room. Everyone has 20 seconds to answer.
          </li>
          <li>A right answer is worth 100 points, plus up to 50 for speed. Not recognising your own song costs 50 points.</li>
          <li>The reveal shows who voted for whom, then a podium with awards such as "The most predictable" or "Musical soulmates".</li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          On the security side, only the game's own site can open a room, and one address cannot create more
          than 10 rooms a minute.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2><span class="pin-name">06</span> Reliability and tests</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <b>The rules are kept apart.</b> Drawing the rounds, scoring and awards are pure functions, with
            no storage and no network: they can be tested on their own.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>A lobby test</b> checks the awkward cases: a foreign site refused, an unknown code, a room full
            at 8, only the host can start, invalid music refused, nicknames cleaned, no secret token visible
            to others, and a phone that reconnects gets its seat back.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>A full-game test</b> plays three simulated players, with shared songs, right and wrong answers,
            a timeout and a phone that reconnects mid-round, up to the podium, whose scores must match the
            lobby's.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>A development tool</b> simulates several phones in a single tab, to test a room without three
            devices at hand.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2><span class="pin-name">07</span> Privacy</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Solo, nothing leaves the device: no account, no server, and the history stays in the browser.</li>
          <li>In a room, only the 15 songs brought and the nickname go through the server, which deletes them 2 hours after the last game.</li>
          <li>Audience measurement uses no cookies and only receives game facts (source, mode, score range), never a name or a song.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2><span class="pin-name">08</span> What I take away</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><b>A complete product, end to end:</b> the idea, the interface, the sources, the server, going live and the documentation.</li>
          <li><b>Real-time multiplayer:</b> an authoritative server, state shared between phones, and tests that replay a whole game.</li>
          <li><b>Working within other people's limits:</b> an API the browser cannot call directly, a sign-in limited to 5 testers, history formats that differ from one service to another.</li>
          <li><b>Privacy by design:</b> whatever can stay on the device stays there.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

